EXECUTED AT RUN TIME (not linked, not redistributed)

  These are separate programs CPU-Power runs to reach root. Neither contributes
  a header to the include graph nor a library to the link line, so neither
  affects this project's MIT licensing -- but they are load-bearing at run time,
  and a NOTICE that only lists what is linked would understate what the tool
  actually depends on.

  polkit                 LGPL-2.0-or-later
  https://github.com/polkit-org/polkit

  Two of its programs are executed with execve(), both found by absolute path
  and never through $PATH:

    /usr/bin/pkexec   starts the privileged helper.
    /usr/bin/pkcheck  is asked, FIRST, whether polkit will authorise the action
                      with no interaction. pkexec is used only if it says yes,
                      which is what keeps this tool from ever raising a password
                      dialog.

  CPU-Power also installs one polkit action (.policy) file describing what the
  local seated user is allowed to do. No polkit header is included and
  libpolkit-gobject is on no link line; scripts/sec-gate.sh asserts this rather
  than trusting it. Behaviour relied on is documented in
  third_party/refs/polkit/SOURCES.md, cited to upstream source by line.

  sudo is NOT executed and no longer appears here. A `sudo -n` fallback existed
  and was removed: a sudoers grant is not limited to the person at the machine,
  so it was a second privilege path with different properties from the first.
  The sudoers example that went with it is deleted too.

